01:01:54,592 --> 01:01:56,962
- The ladder truck is the best. - Right. It's the best thing.

1551
01:01:57,892 --> 01:01:59,322
(Empty)

1552
01:01:59,322 --> 01:02:00,332
Gosh.

1553
01:02:01,062 --> 01:02:03,932
(He checks the empty space without the sofa.)

1554
01:02:05,032 --> 01:02:07,872
You must've gotten attached to it with the dog you raised.

1555
01:02:07,872 --> 01:02:10,502
Right. That is why I'm trying to change everything.

1556
01:02:10,902 --> 01:02:11,942
Right.

1557
01:02:12,572 --> 01:02:14,912
Gosh, goodbye!

1558
01:02:15,412 --> 01:02:17,112
You get attached to furniture.

1559
01:02:17,382 --> 01:02:19,142
- I got attached to it. - Right.

1560
01:02:19,142 --> 01:02:20,412
It feels strange when you throw out your furniture.

1561
01:02:20,412 --> 01:02:21,512
- It felt strange. - Right.

1562
01:02:21,512 --> 01:02:22,882
- Right. - You get attached to furniture.

1563
01:02:24,952 --> 01:02:26,252
It must be coming up.

1564
01:02:27,992 --> 01:02:30,062
Hold on. Okay. I'll pull it.

1565
01:02:30,622 --> 01:02:31,892
Gosh, this is also heavy.

1566
01:02:31,892 --> 01:02:33,492
Right. We'll hold it from this side.

1567
01:02:34,092 --> 01:02:35,132
There we go.

1568
01:02:35,932 --> 01:02:37,762
(It gets placed at the same spot of the previous sofa.)

1569
01:02:37,762 --> 01:02:39,132
There we go! Thank you.

1570
01:02:39,302 --> 01:02:40,432
That's perfect.

1571
01:02:40,902 --> 01:02:43,172
(Letting go of the sofa that seemed like it got 10,000 shots)

1572
01:02:43,602 --> 01:02:44,602
(Done changing into a new sofa in the same style)

1573
01:02:44,602 --> 01:02:45,942
He got the same sofa.

1574
01:02:45,942 --> 01:02:47,572
- Why did you get the same one? - I mean...

1575
01:02:47,842 --> 01:02:49,212
It must be really important...

1576
01:02:49,212 --> 01:02:51,142
- to have a cup holder for you. - Of course.

1577
01:02:51,142 --> 01:02:53,252
I'm also very sensitive about having storage...

1578
01:02:53,252 --> 01:02:54,952
- on both sides of the sofa. - That one has everything.

1579
01:02:55,382 --> 01:02:56,952
- There's a storage at the bottom. - That's right.

1580
01:02:56,952 --> 01:02:58,922
- Because you need to store food. - It needs a storage.

1581
01:02:59,282 --> 01:03:01,722
- We'll take a photo of the sofa. - Sure.

1582
01:03:04,192 --> 01:03:05,822
- Just the sofa, please. - Oh, just the sofa?

1583
01:03:06,932 --> 01:03:09,032
- Just the sofa, please. - Oh, just the sofa?

1584
01:03:09,162 --> 01:03:10,302
Oh, okay.

1585
01:03:10,362 --> 01:03:12,262
- That was... - My gosh. That day...

1586
01:03:12,762 --> 01:03:14,502
That's really embarrassing.

1587
01:03:14,802 --> 01:03:16,942
- Thank you. - He's acting like a total celeb.

1588
01:03:18,102 --> 01:03:19,572
- Thank you. - Bye.

1589
01:03:19,572 --> 01:03:20,572
(Saying goodbye to the installer awkwardly)

1590
01:03:21,042 --> 01:03:23,882
Gosh, this... This is crazy, seriously.

1591
01:03:24,382 --> 01:03:26,242
(Plopping down)

1592
01:03:26,682 --> 01:03:28,312
(Amazed)

1593
01:03:28,312 --> 01:03:29,712
- That must be nice. - That's nice.

1594
01:03:31,182 --> 01:03:33,522
(Super satisfied)

1595
01:03:34,022 --> 01:03:35,052
Happiness is found...

1596
01:03:35,052 --> 01:03:36,052
- in the simplest things. - Of course.

1597
01:03:36,492 --> 01:03:38,392
My goodness. This is nice.

1598
01:03:39,192 --> 01:03:40,862
Since I've cleaned the house...

1599
01:03:40,862 --> 01:03:42,192
(As he's refreshed...)

1600
01:03:43,732 --> 01:03:45,332
Is he going to do something there?

1601
01:03:46,332 --> 01:03:47,402
Right around here.

1602
01:03:47,502 --> 01:03:49,402
I always leave the windows open every day.

1603
01:03:49,502 --> 01:03:50,842
- I don't like feeling stuffy. - That's right.

1604
01:03:50,842 --> 01:03:52,442
So there's so much dust on the balcony.

1605
01:03:53,072 --> 01:03:55,472
Since more dust came in with the new furniture,

1606
01:03:56,012 --> 01:03:57,912
I decided to clean the balcony.

1607
01:03:57,912 --> 01:03:59,182
(Planning to clean the balcony thoroughly)

1608
01:04:00,052 --> 01:04:02,582
Gosh, this isn't some kind of barbecue restaurant.

1609
01:04:03,422 --> 01:04:04,482
- Why is there... - Gosh.

1610
01:04:04,482 --> 01:04:05,822
- I was really shocked. - The grill plate...

1611
01:04:05,822 --> 01:04:06,922
- It's fantastic. - You have all kinds...

1612
01:04:06,922 --> 01:04:08,652
- of grill plates. - Gosh, it's a cauldron lid.

1613
01:04:08,652 --> 01:04:10,062
(Here's another grill plate.)

1614
01:04:11,192 --> 01:04:12,192
You can make doenjang jjigae with it.

1615
01:04:12,192 --> 01:04:13,292
Doenjang jjigae? You know it.

1616
01:04:13,892 --> 01:04:15,692
- How many grill plates do you have? - This is crazy.

1617
01:04:16,032 --> 01:04:17,832
Do you use different plates depending on your mood?

1618
01:04:17,932 --> 01:04:20,602
- No, it depends on the meat. - It depends on the cuts.

1619
01:04:21,302 --> 01:04:22,632
(Filling the washbin with water...)

1620
01:04:22,632 --> 01:04:23,972
(after removing all the stuff on the balcony)

1621
01:04:25,742 --> 01:04:27,672
(Then he adds dish soap into it.)

1622
01:04:27,672 --> 01:04:30,182
I highly recommend diluting dish soap...

1623
01:04:30,482 --> 01:04:33,142
into water when you do cleaning.

1624
01:04:33,512 --> 01:04:35,852
I've tried using everything like baking soda, vinegar,

1625
01:04:35,852 --> 01:04:37,922
and bleach.

1626
01:04:38,082 --> 01:04:41,552
I think it's best to use dish soap.

1627
01:04:41,752 --> 01:04:43,792
- Is that so? - That's right.

1628
01:04:43,992 --> 01:04:45,392
Oh, really? Like that?

1629
01:04:45,862 --> 01:04:47,132
I'm lifting things a lot today.

1630
01:04:47,992 --> 01:04:49,692
(To the balcony with a washbin filled with dish soap water)

1631
01:04:49,692 --> 01:04:50,802
Gosh!

1632
01:04:51,462 --> 01:04:55,432
(Stubborn grime stuck to the floor)

1633
01:04:56,332 --> 01:04:57,742
(I'll clean them all today.)

1634
01:04:57,742 --> 01:04:58,942
You're going to clean them.

1635
01:04:58,942 --> 01:05:00,842
- For cleaning the floor? - That's right.

1636
01:05:02,072 --> 01:05:03,512
Gosh, how refreshing.

1637
01:05:04,442 --> 01:05:07,352
- You feel great when you do that. - Yes, when you spray water.

1638
01:05:08,112 --> 01:05:09,282
Gosh, this is nice.

1639
01:05:12,982 --> 01:05:14,092
Oh, you're using your hands?

1640
01:05:14,622 --> 01:05:16,352
- No, using an exfoliating towel. - An exfoliating towel?

1641
01:05:16,552 --> 01:05:18,862
- I'm using that because... - You know your stuff.

1642
01:05:18,862 --> 01:05:20,132
you can clean every nook and cranny...

1643
01:05:20,132 --> 01:05:21,432
- compared to using a brush... - That's right.

1644
01:05:21,432 --> 01:05:22,662
because you can use your fingers.

1645
01:05:24,032 --> 01:05:27,302
(Scrubbing hard)

1646
01:05:27,872 --> 01:05:29,532
(Sung Hwan exfoliates every nook and cranny.)

1647
01:05:29,532 --> 01:05:31,402
I feel great when I clean like that because...

1648
01:05:31,402 --> 01:05:33,242
- The grime gets washed away. - the grime gets washed away.

1649
01:05:33,242 --> 01:05:36,112
(Feeling refreshed)

1650
01:05:36,442 --> 01:05:37,612
Look at the built-up grime.

1651
01:05:39,182 --> 01:05:41,082
(Hopping)

1652
01:05:41,082 --> 01:05:42,612
- That must be really slippery. - It is.

1653
01:05:42,612 --> 01:05:43,822
That's why you need to squat down.

1654
01:05:43,822 --> 01:05:44,922
This feels great.

1655
01:05:45,682 --> 01:05:47,192
Gosh, it gets washed off well.

1656
01:05:47,552 --> 01:05:49,152
- A steel wool scrubber too? - With the towel...

1657
01:05:49,152 --> 01:05:50,392
and steel wool scrubber...

1658
01:05:51,562 --> 01:05:52,822
(Sliding)

1659
01:05:59,462 --> 01:06:00,502
(Slipping)

1660
01:06:00,502 --> 01:06:01,602
- Oh, no! - Goodness!

1661
01:06:02,102 --> 01:06:03,872
- Your head! - Watch your head!

1662
01:06:03,872 --> 01:06:05,372
My gosh!

1663
01:06:05,372 --> 01:06:06,472
You almost died.

1664
01:06:06,572 --> 01:06:09,112
You survived because you gained fat on your back. Otherwise...

1665
01:06:09,242 --> 01:06:11,112
If it were CODE KUNST, he'd have hit the back of his head.

1666
01:06:11,112 --> 01:06:12,342
- If it were CODE KUNST, - The back of his head...

1667
01:06:12,342 --> 01:06:13,412
- he'd have broken his back. - That was dangerous.

1668
01:06:13,412 --> 01:06:14,852
I hold a third-degree black belt in judo.

1669
01:06:15,382 --> 01:06:17,952
Those who did judo like me and Shin Young...

1670
01:06:17,952 --> 01:06:19,422
Because I did judo,

1671
01:06:19,422 --> 01:06:20,792
- I did a breakfall right away. - That wasn't a breakfall.

1672
01:06:20,792 --> 01:06:22,292
- You just fell. - You were shocked.

1673
01:06:22,392 --> 01:06:24,722
- It wasn't a breakfall. - It wasn't. You just slipped.

1674
01:06:26,222 --> 01:06:27,232
A breakfall.

1675
01:06:28,162 --> 01:06:31,462
(What just happened?)

1676
01:06:35,232 --> 01:06:36,402
Gosh, the breakfall.

1677
01:06:36,802 --> 01:06:37,872
Thanks to the breakfall...

1678
01:06:40,072 --> 01:06:41,772
If I hadn't learned how to do breakfalls,

1679
01:06:42,212 --> 01:06:43,372
I'd have died.

1680
01:06:46,882 --> 01:06:50,052
(Shall I rinse off now?)

1681
01:06:52,022 --> 01:06:53,852
(Splash)

1682
01:06:55,292 --> 01:06:57,092
You should've connected a hose.

1683
01:06:57,392 --> 01:06:58,422
I can't connect a hose.

1684
01:06:58,422 --> 01:07:00,392
- There's no... - No. In the sink,

1685
01:07:00,392 --> 01:07:01,532
- In the sink... - there's a water supply line.

1686
01:07:01,532 --> 01:07:02,762
- You can connect a hose there. - Yes.

1687
01:07:03,292 --> 01:07:05,032
- Because that's a lot of work. - When he does cleaning next time,

1688
01:07:05,032 --> 01:07:06,202
CODE KUNST should visit.

1689
01:07:06,202 --> 01:07:07,872
You can give me orders.

1690
01:07:07,872 --> 01:07:09,732
- I will... - Then I'll share my knowledge.

1691
01:07:09,732 --> 01:07:11,242
No. You can share knowledge too,

1692
01:07:11,602 --> 01:07:13,072
- but just tell me what to do. - Oh, I can...

1693
01:07:13,072 --> 01:07:14,942
- just tell you what to do? - Yes, on the sofa.

1694
01:07:14,942 --> 01:07:15,942
Sounds good.

1695
01:07:15,942 --> 01:07:17,512
Until it dries...

1696
01:07:17,512 --> 01:07:20,252
(Let me wash until the floor dries.)

1697
01:07:20,252 --> 01:07:21,612
Let me wash.

1698
01:07:23,282 --> 01:07:25,082
It feels refreshing.

1699
01:07:25,252 --> 01:07:26,322
It's so clean.

1700
01:07:26,322 --> 01:07:27,322
It's shining.

1701
01:07:27,692 --> 01:07:29,622
- That gives you so much pleasure. - That's amazing.

1702
01:07:31,662 --> 01:07:32,662
(Sung Hwan is also washing and polishing up.)

1703
01:07:32,662 --> 01:07:34,462
It'll be dried fast as the windows are open.

1704
01:07:34,462 --> 01:07:35,592
It dries off soon.

1705
01:07:39,732 --> 01:07:41,202
Gosh, how refreshing.

1706
01:07:41,202 --> 01:07:42,372
(Feeling refreshed)

1707
01:07:42,372 --> 01:07:45,642
(Humming)

1708
01:07:47,672 --> 01:07:48,772
Honey

1709
01:07:48,772 --> 01:07:51,412
(Digging through)

1710
01:07:51,412 --> 01:07:52,642
You too...

1711
01:07:54,212 --> 01:07:55,712
Let's go.

1712
01:07:56,852 --> 01:07:58,182
Let's go.

1713
01:07:58,952 --> 01:08:00,022
Let's go.

1714
01:08:00,022 --> 01:08:01,192
(Going outside with a bunch of clothes)

1715
01:08:01,192 --> 01:08:02,392
Honey

1716
01:08:03,352 --> 01:08:04,522
Goodness.

1717
01:08:09,692 --> 01:08:11,502
Write down the registration number on a piece of paper.

1718
01:08:11,502 --> 01:08:12,862
Where's the registration number? Oh, here it is.

1719
01:08:12,862 --> 01:08:14,832
(He took the disposal registration number before leaving.)

1720
01:08:14,832 --> 01:08:18,172
Two, two, three. All right.

1721
01:08:18,972 --> 01:08:21,412
(He stuck it on the mattress and sofa without forgetting.)

1722
01:08:21,412 --> 01:08:22,442
- Oh, you can just do that? - Right.

1723
01:08:22,442 --> 01:08:23,442
People write the numbers these days.

1724
01:08:23,442 --> 01:08:24,682
- You need to write down the number. - Right.

1725
01:08:24,682 --> 01:08:26,382
- That's nice. - We used to put a sticker on it.

1726
01:08:26,382 --> 01:08:27,512
- I know. - Yes.

1727
01:08:30,652 --> 01:08:32,782
- Wait. You're here. - I'm here.

1728
01:08:33,222 --> 01:08:34,692
I thought you had a field day.

1729
01:08:34,692 --> 01:08:37,162
(Sung Hwan is greeting someone warmly?)

1730
01:08:38,438 --> 01:08:39,638
- Wait. - I'm here.

1731
01:08:39,638 --> 01:08:40,738
You're here.

1732
01:08:41,138 --> 01:08:42,578
I thought you had a field day.

1733
01:08:42,848 --> 01:08:45,448
- Didn't you have a field day? - No, tomorrow... Today...

1734
01:08:45,708 --> 01:08:47,148
We just trained today.

1735
01:08:47,618 --> 01:08:49,278
Then we timed it.

1736
01:08:49,918 --> 01:08:50,988
Who is that?

1737
01:08:51,118 --> 01:08:52,348
That's right. It's him.

1738
01:08:52,688 --> 01:08:54,258
- Mr. Ddol. - It's Mr. Ddol.

1739
01:08:54,258 --> 01:08:55,258
(I remember him!)

1740
01:08:55,258 --> 01:08:57,228
(He met Mr. Ddol next door last time too!)

1741
01:08:58,388 --> 01:08:59,958
- Then tomorrow... - He became fitter.

1742
01:08:59,958 --> 01:09:01,998
- Yes. - He looks firmer.

1743
01:09:02,258 --> 01:09:03,798
Let's go ride together next time.

1744
01:09:04,068 --> 01:09:05,568
How far do you go?

1745
01:09:05,568 --> 01:09:06,698
I'll go wherever you want.

1746
01:09:06,698 --> 01:09:09,268
- You think you can handle me? - Yes.

1747
01:09:09,938 --> 01:09:12,108
- "You think you can handle me?" - I'm practically an ironman.

1748
01:09:12,108 --> 01:09:13,378
(I'm practically an ironman.)

1749
01:09:13,378 --> 01:09:15,338
Let's go ride together when the weather gets better.

1750
01:09:15,538 --> 01:09:16,578
- I'll get going now. - Goodbye.

1751
01:09:16,578 --> 01:09:18,008
You say that to everyone you meet.

1752
01:09:18,008 --> 01:09:19,078
"You think you can handle me?"

1753
01:09:21,978 --> 01:09:24,088
- Hello, sir. - Hey.

1754
01:09:24,718 --> 01:09:25,948
- You're well, right? - Yes.

1755
01:09:26,088 --> 01:09:27,218
It's been so long.

1756
01:09:27,958 --> 01:09:30,188
- My hands are full, so... - Hey.

1757
01:09:30,188 --> 01:09:31,688
- He's like the unofficial mayor. - I know.

1758
01:09:31,688 --> 01:09:33,298
They're all neighborhood guys to me.

1759
01:09:33,298 --> 01:09:34,358
- A selfie... - Yes.

1760
01:09:34,358 --> 01:09:35,628
- Stay healthy, sir. - Sure.

1761
01:09:35,628 --> 01:09:37,028
I'll call you. Bye.

1762
01:09:37,228 --> 01:09:38,398
Gosh, I haven't seen him in a long while.

1763
01:09:38,768 --> 01:09:40,738
Forty-six years? That means he's...

1764
01:09:40,738 --> 01:09:42,098
- That's right. - Yes.

1765
01:09:43,908 --> 01:09:46,938
(He kept walking while saying hi to his neighbors.)

1766
01:09:47,408 --> 01:09:48,708
- Hello. - Goodness.

1767
01:09:50,108 --> 01:09:53,078
(He said hi to her last summer as well.)

1768
01:09:53,718 --> 01:09:58,488
(Sung Hwan's go-to dry cleaner)

1769
01:09:59,248 --> 01:10:01,058
- This needs to be dry-cleaned. - Yes.

1770
01:10:01,058 --> 01:10:03,358
This needs to be ironed.

1771
01:10:03,358 --> 01:10:04,428
I think you have...

1772
01:10:04,428 --> 01:10:05,428
- one of my clothes here. - Right.

1773
01:10:05,428 --> 01:10:06,828
I'll take them together when these are done.

1774
01:10:06,828 --> 01:10:08,028
- Together? - Yes, together.

1775
01:10:08,028 --> 01:10:10,568
- I should've brought it. - Have two of these.

1776
01:10:10,568 --> 01:10:11,698
An ice pop.

1777
01:10:11,928 --> 01:10:13,138
Have one of this too.

1778
01:10:13,138 --> 01:10:15,068
Gosh, you're so warm-hearted.

1779
01:10:15,568 --> 01:10:18,138
They always give me lots of things...

1780
01:10:18,638 --> 01:10:20,008
- like food when I visit. - Goodness.

1781
01:10:20,008 --> 01:10:21,808
Then I'd give them some whenever I go there.

1782
01:10:22,238 --> 01:10:23,308
And this.

1783
01:10:23,308 --> 01:10:25,808
(He shares ice cream and bread today like usual.)

1784
01:10:25,808 --> 01:10:26,948
This one.

1785
01:10:26,948 --> 01:10:28,078
Gosh, you shouldn't.

1786
01:10:28,078 --> 01:10:29,448
(Sung Hwan shares generously.)

1787
01:10:29,748 --> 01:10:31,148
Something smells nice.

1788
01:10:31,418 --> 01:10:33,188
I made some toast for lunch.

1789
01:10:33,188 --> 01:10:34,288
Would you like one?

1790
01:10:34,588 --> 01:10:36,158
- Toast? - I made a lot.

1791
01:10:36,158 --> 01:10:37,328
- Toast? - Yes.

1792
01:10:37,328 --> 01:10:38,628
Then shall I taste it a little?

1793
01:10:38,728 --> 01:10:40,798
Don't just eat a little. Eat a lot.

1794
01:10:40,958 --> 01:10:42,128
You should eat. That's right.

1795
01:10:42,128 --> 01:10:44,098
- You like such food the most. - I do.

1796
01:10:44,098 --> 01:10:45,668
- Toast, burger... - A burger, yes.

1797
01:10:45,798 --> 01:10:47,038
I had a burger today.

1798
01:10:47,568 --> 01:10:48,698
Gosh.

1799
01:10:48,938 --> 01:10:50,038
Gosh, this is nice.

1800
01:10:51,408 --> 01:10:52,608
I mean...

1801
01:10:53,108 --> 01:10:54,338
Gosh, toast...

1802
01:10:54,708 --> 01:10:55,738
- That's... - Gosh.

1803
01:10:56,038 --> 01:10:58,108
- Gosh, it's the classic toast. - Homemade toast.

1804
01:10:58,108 --> 01:11:00,018
- It looks so good. Right? - You know, right?

1805
01:11:00,548 --> 01:11:03,018
- With margarine... - Margarine, yes. Exactly.

1806
01:11:03,018 --> 01:11:04,148
With lots of sugar.

1807
01:11:04,448 --> 01:11:07,718
When I come into your hideout...

1808
01:11:07,718 --> 01:11:09,258
Gosh! Look at this.

1809
01:11:09,258 --> 01:11:10,258
(The lady takes something out from the fridge.)

1810
01:11:10,558 --> 01:11:13,758
Is this "Under the Same Roof" from the 1980s?

1811
01:11:13,958 --> 01:11:15,728
- This is... - That's the best with toast.

1812
01:11:15,728 --> 01:11:17,128
So it was real.

1813
01:11:17,128 --> 01:11:19,098
- It's a must-have combination. - I like old-style bottles.

1814
01:11:19,098 --> 01:11:20,438
Then I will...

1815
01:11:20,438 --> 01:11:21,738
Since she gave it to me, even though she made it for the meal,

1816
01:11:22,468 --> 01:11:23,738
- I should eat it with gusto. - This is great.

1817
01:11:25,138 --> 01:11:28,238
(Taking a big bite)

1818
01:11:28,878 --> 01:11:30,178
- It's so good. - It's good, right?

1819
01:11:30,178 --> 01:11:31,308
- It's so good. - Do you want me to cut it?

1820
01:11:31,578 --> 01:11:32,778
It's nothing fancy,

1821
01:11:32,778 --> 01:11:34,018
- but it's really good. - That's the best.

1822
01:11:34,018 --> 01:11:35,478
It's not because I'm hungry.

1823
01:11:36,148 --> 01:11:37,448
It's savory, indeed.

1824
01:11:37,448 --> 01:11:39,288
(Adding sugar on top this time)

1825
01:11:39,718 --> 01:11:41,488
- You should have some. - With sugar.

1826
01:11:41,488 --> 01:11:43,388
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1827
01:11:43,488 --> 01:11:45,828
How did you know that I made some toast?

1828
01:11:45,928 --> 01:11:46,998
I didn't know.

1829
01:11:48,828 --> 01:11:49,868
It won't change now?

1830
01:11:50,598 --> 01:11:54,038
That dish and the drink...

1831
01:11:54,038 --> 01:11:55,968
- They're all classic. - That's right.

1832
01:11:55,968 --> 01:11:57,838
- They're the originals. - Yes, totally.

1833
01:11:57,838 --> 01:12:00,108
- Totally. It's so nice to see. - That's the best.

1834
01:12:00,208 --> 01:12:01,338
It's therapeutic.

1835
01:12:02,238 --> 01:12:03,348
How are your knees?

1836
01:12:03,408 --> 01:12:06,718
They are getting better. Because I lost weight...

1837
01:12:06,948 --> 01:12:08,218
- Right. - When I went trekking,

1838
01:12:08,218 --> 01:12:09,448
it was a little hard.

1839
01:12:09,448 --> 01:12:11,888
The knee cartilage...

1840
01:12:11,888 --> 01:12:13,388
was torn.

1841
01:12:13,388 --> 01:12:15,918
I walked with the torn cartilage, so it was hard.

1842
01:12:15,918 --> 01:12:18,788
After seeing you, I wanted to give it a try. These days,

1843
01:12:18,888 --> 01:12:20,158
I'm working really hard.

1844
01:12:20,328 --> 01:12:22,128
I'm working really hard, but it's not that easy.

1845
01:12:22,128 --> 01:12:24,328
You look a lot better...

1846
01:12:24,328 --> 01:12:26,128
- than the last time I saw you. - Really?

1847
01:12:26,128 --> 01:12:27,338
- You definitely look better. - He lost a bit of weight.

1848
01:12:27,338 --> 01:12:28,498
- It looks like he did. - He did lose weight.

1849
01:12:29,298 --> 01:12:32,838
You lost so much weight. You look gaunt.

1850
01:12:33,038 --> 01:12:34,708
Even after losing weight, I'm still 95kg.

1851
01:12:34,978 --> 01:12:37,048
- But you're big-built. - You're big-built.

1852
01:12:37,278 --> 01:12:38,478
You're big-built.

1853
01:12:38,478 --> 01:12:40,278
- But... - You're big-framed.

1854
01:12:41,578 --> 01:12:43,988
Because they're old, they called me big-framed.

1855
01:12:44,348 --> 01:12:45,818
I'll take this from home.

1856
01:12:46,218 --> 01:12:49,058
(The knees of a big-framed man are creaky.)

1857
01:12:49,188 --> 01:12:50,658
- Gosh, ma'am. - Goodness.

1858
01:12:50,658 --> 01:12:52,128
I enjoyed it so much. What do I do?

1859
01:12:53,058 --> 01:12:54,298
What do you mean?

1860
01:12:54,298 --> 01:12:55,498
- It's good that you enjoyed it. - Goodness.

1861
01:12:55,498 --> 01:12:56,828
I always get treated by you,

1862
01:12:56,828 --> 01:12:58,168
- like ramyeon. - No.

1863
01:12:59,228 --> 01:13:00,268
I'll come pick it up...

1864
01:13:00,268 --> 01:13:01,298
- when the rest are done. - Sure.

1865
01:13:01,298 --> 01:13:02,768
- Thank you. - Bye.

1866
01:13:02,838 --> 01:13:04,068
- Bye. - Bye. Thank you.

1867
01:13:04,808 --> 01:13:06,038
Gosh, the weather...

1868
01:13:07,708 --> 01:13:10,208
The weather... I hope it doesn't rain.

1869
01:13:11,608 --> 01:13:14,278
(He arrives home after taking a walk around the neighborhood.)

1870
01:13:14,278 --> 01:13:16,688
Gosh, I'm starving.

1871
01:13:21,658 --> 01:13:23,728
(Taking it)

1872
01:13:25,028 --> 01:13:26,458
(He took out a huge tray like usual.)

1873
01:13:26,458 --> 01:13:29,228
- And then... - You must have a tray like that...

1874
01:13:29,228 --> 01:13:30,568
for your convenience.

1875
01:13:34,798 --> 01:13:36,938
(Opening the fridge after taking out the tray)

1876
01:13:37,638 --> 01:13:39,068
(Picking)

1877
01:13:39,068 --> 01:13:40,078
This and...

1878
01:13:40,078 --> 01:13:42,938
(Picking)

1879
01:13:44,048 --> 01:13:47,018
(He carefully selected the ingredients for dinner.)

1880
01:13:47,018 --> 01:13:48,418
Let's make it delicious.

1881
01:13:49,418 --> 01:13:52,018
(Rummaging)

1882
01:13:54,788 --> 01:13:57,928
Wait. Is he making budae jjigae? Or tteokbokki?

1883
01:13:58,358 --> 01:13:59,358
All right.

1884
01:13:59,458 --> 01:14:02,798
(Getting fully prepared with an apron and gloves)

1885
01:14:04,268 --> 01:14:06,398
(This is great.)

1886
01:14:07,938 --> 01:14:09,768
- One-pot tteokbokki. - Gosh. That's it.

1887
01:14:10,468 --> 01:14:12,338
- What's that? Vertically... - You can tell from the pot alone?

1888
01:14:12,338 --> 01:14:15,008
Wait. You should make it vertical.

1889
01:14:17,308 --> 01:14:18,748
- Jjajang? - It's black bean paste.

1890
01:14:19,548 --> 01:14:20,578
You're making jjajang tteokbokki?

1891
01:14:23,218 --> 01:14:24,218
Adding chili oil too.

1892
01:14:24,218 --> 01:14:25,218
(Pouring chili oil on top)

1893
01:14:25,818 --> 01:14:30,088
I bought garaetteok on the way home.

1894
01:14:30,228 --> 01:14:31,888
It's rabokki with brisket, jjajang,

1895
01:14:32,658 --> 01:14:35,298
garaetteok, udon, and ramyeon.

1896
01:14:35,298 --> 01:14:37,928
- What? It's ramyeon and what? - You're eating all those?

1897
01:14:39,498 --> 01:14:42,598
BJ... BJG...

1898
01:14:43,268 --> 01:14:46,238
BJGRU rabokki?

1899
01:14:46,238 --> 01:14:48,438
It's BJGRU rabokki.

1900
01:14:48,438 --> 01:14:49,538
BJGRU?

1901
01:14:51,478 --> 01:14:54,618
(Let's stir-fry it well.)

1902
01:14:56,278 --> 01:14:58,688
(Stirring)

1903
01:14:59,318 --> 01:15:01,188
The black bean paste tastes better if you stir-fry it in oil.

1904
01:15:01,388 --> 01:15:03,128
- With chili oil, it's even better. - That's right.

1905
01:15:03,128 --> 01:15:05,288
With a sharp kick.

1906
01:15:05,388 --> 01:15:06,388
It's all set.

1907
01:15:06,388 --> 01:15:07,528
(Grabbing the brisket)

1908
01:15:07,528 --> 01:15:09,728
- Gosh, brisket. - It's better to use brisket...

1909
01:15:09,728 --> 01:15:10,728
- because it has more oil... - That's right.

1910
01:15:10,728 --> 01:15:12,068
than other meat.

1911
01:15:12,468 --> 01:15:13,698
- The savory oil... - That's right.

1912
01:15:13,698 --> 01:15:15,268
from beef comes out.

1913
01:15:16,298 --> 01:15:17,808
Your life and mine...

1914
01:15:17,808 --> 01:15:18,908
- are similar. - They're similar.

1915
01:15:18,908 --> 01:15:20,238
- They're really similar. - They're the same.

1916
01:15:20,978 --> 01:15:23,038
(After adding lots of brisket...)

1917
01:15:23,348 --> 01:15:25,648
(Sprinkling chili powder that will cut through the greasiness)

1918
01:15:26,278 --> 01:15:29,118
(Adding green onion and garlic according to his taste)

1919
01:15:29,848 --> 01:15:31,548
(Amazed)

1920
01:15:31,548 --> 01:15:32,848
The smell is crazy good.

1921
01:15:34,358 --> 01:15:35,358
It looks good.

1922
01:15:37,328 --> 01:15:38,758
Cut it thinly.

1923
01:15:38,758 --> 01:15:41,228
That way, the meat juice comes out, and the taste becomes richer.

1924
01:15:42,498 --> 01:15:45,768
If you boil the ramyeon noodles separately and mix them with it,

1925
01:15:45,768 --> 01:15:48,398
that becomes jjajangmyeon.

1926
01:15:50,768 --> 01:15:51,808
Add garaetteok.

1927
01:15:51,808 --> 01:15:55,308
(Adding garaetteok and fish cake on top of the stir-fried sauce)

1928
01:15:56,108 --> 01:15:57,748
You must add ramyeon seasoning.

1929
01:15:57,748 --> 01:15:59,278
Yes, ramyeon seasoning.

1930
01:16:00,118 --> 01:16:02,378
(Adding lots of pepper, which he likes)

1931
01:16:02,948 --> 01:16:05,088
- You must add pepper. - Pepper is a must.

1932
01:16:06,988 --> 01:16:08,688
(Egg and cheese)

1933
01:16:08,688 --> 01:16:10,388
(He doesn't stop adding ingredients.)

1934
01:16:10,388 --> 01:16:11,428
You must add cheese.

1935
01:16:12,158 --> 01:16:14,528
(Lastly, he pours an ox bone broth.)

1936
01:16:14,558 --> 01:16:16,798
Gosh, look how chili oil rises up.

1937
01:16:17,898 --> 01:16:18,898
That's it.

1938
01:16:18,898 --> 01:16:20,168
(BJGRU rabokki is ready.)

1939
01:16:20,868 --> 01:16:23,038
(Dinner prep is all done.)

1940
01:16:25,838 --> 01:16:28,478
(Pulling out a wet wipe)

1941
01:16:29,538 --> 01:16:31,108
(Wiping)

1942
01:16:31,108 --> 01:16:33,678
If chili oil splatters,

1943
01:16:33,678 --> 01:16:35,718
- That's right. - it could leave grease stains.

1944
01:16:35,718 --> 01:16:36,718
- I feel... - I must wipe it right away.

1945
01:16:36,718 --> 01:16:37,948
Watching you...

1946
01:16:38,488 --> 01:16:39,618
made me the most relaxed.

1947
01:16:39,618 --> 01:16:40,818
(Bonding over cleaning after cooking)

1948
01:16:41,218 --> 01:16:42,488
Gosh!

1949
01:16:43,158 --> 01:16:44,288
(The chili oil splattered.)

1950
01:16:44,288 --> 01:16:45,828
I was already annoyed.

1951
01:16:45,828 --> 01:16:48,628
Isn't that okay?

1952
01:16:49,328 --> 01:16:50,868
You can't wipe it off if it stays for too long.

1953
01:16:50,928 --> 01:16:53,098
If it stays, no matter how hard I wipe,

1954
01:16:53,098 --> 01:16:54,168
- That's right. - it smears.

1955
01:16:54,168 --> 01:16:56,838
- That's right. - Yes. I agree with that.

1956
01:16:57,638 --> 01:17:00,638
(He quickly took out a mini scrub brush before it smears.)

1957
01:17:03,038 --> 01:17:04,548
Oh, he can't even stand that.

1958
01:17:04,548 --> 01:17:05,608
My gosh.

1959
01:17:08,318 --> 01:17:10,418
This is nice. It's so relaxing.

1960
01:17:10,418 --> 01:17:12,888
Even if you wipe like that, it takes less than five minutes.

1961
01:17:12,888 --> 01:17:14,988
It's nothing shocking. It takes less than five minutes.

1962
01:17:14,988 --> 01:17:16,358
It takes no time.

1963
01:17:16,958 --> 01:17:18,158
All right. It's all set.

1964
01:17:18,158 --> 01:17:19,158
(Clean)

1965
01:17:19,528 --> 01:17:21,628
(Rummaging)

1966
01:17:22,628 --> 01:17:23,728
(A handheld portable fan?)

1967
01:17:23,728 --> 01:17:25,598
- Gosh, for real? - No way.

1968
01:17:27,298 --> 01:17:31,438
(Drying off water with a handheld portable fan)

1969
01:17:32,538 --> 01:17:34,578
(Shining without a single stain)

1970
01:17:34,578 --> 01:17:36,278
It looks like he polished it to a shine.

1971
01:17:36,538 --> 01:17:37,648
It looks new.

1972
01:17:38,208 --> 01:17:39,508
All set! Perfect.

1973
01:17:39,848 --> 01:17:41,018
If it gets messy, you can wipe it right away.

1974
01:17:41,018 --> 01:17:42,178
That's the most important.

1975
01:17:42,518 --> 01:17:45,218
But what's even more important is drying it.

1976
01:17:45,548 --> 01:17:46,658
If you don't dry it,

1977
01:17:46,658 --> 01:17:47,758
- it becomes stained. - That's right.

1978
01:17:48,018 --> 01:17:50,588
It feels nice if it shines.

1979
01:17:50,928 --> 01:17:53,298
Try this. Wipe it with a dry towel,

1980
01:17:53,298 --> 01:17:55,858
and then use a dishcloth...

1981
01:17:55,928 --> 01:17:57,798
that absorbs water at once.

1982
01:17:57,998 --> 01:17:59,598
- If you use that once... - Oh, I do wipe with it.

1983
01:17:59,598 --> 01:18:01,638
It doesn't leave fingerprints. You don't have to do that.

1984
01:18:01,868 --> 01:18:04,208
Still, I do that just in case.

1985
01:18:04,208 --> 01:18:06,068
I see. It doesn't take long.

1986
01:18:06,068 --> 01:18:07,638
- The thing is... - Or leave the range hood on.

1987
01:18:07,638 --> 01:18:09,138
- If you listen to them carefully, - I left it on.

1988
01:18:09,138 --> 01:18:10,648
they don't listen to each other at all.

1989
01:18:11,548 --> 01:18:13,848
- They keep going on. - I mean, in the end...

1990
01:18:13,848 --> 01:18:16,078
- We're just saying what we want. - They're trying to beat each other.

1991
01:18:16,078 --> 01:18:17,548
- No, that's not true. - They wouldn't agree.

1992
01:18:17,548 --> 01:18:19,488
- We're on different wavelengths. - We are...

1993
01:18:19,488 --> 01:18:21,088
- It's more like sharing info. - Yes, we're sharing info.

1994
01:18:21,088 --> 01:18:22,118
"That's true, but..."

1995
01:18:22,718 --> 01:18:24,858
(Lifting the tray filled with ingredients)

1996
01:18:24,858 --> 01:18:26,028
All right.

1997
01:18:26,388 --> 01:18:29,328
(I should go up since I'm done cleaning up.)

1998
01:18:30,028 --> 01:18:32,328
(Careful)

1999
01:18:38,738 --> 01:18:40,338
(Heave-ho)

2000
01:18:40,338 --> 01:18:42,078
- Gosh. - This is almost like...

2001
01:18:42,408 --> 01:18:44,208
You have a table used in restaurants.

2002
01:18:44,208 --> 01:18:46,348
I have many things that are used in restaurants.

2003
01:18:47,718 --> 01:18:48,778
All right.

2004
01:18:48,778 --> 01:18:49,878
(Placing the pot on the gas stove)

2005
01:18:50,348 --> 01:18:52,388
(Clicking)

2006
01:18:53,618 --> 01:18:56,018
(The heat is turned on.)

2007
01:18:56,858 --> 01:18:58,828
(This is good.)

2008
01:18:59,528 --> 01:19:01,858
(Soon, the broth starts boiling.)

2009
01:19:02,758 --> 01:19:04,898
(Grabbing ramyeon)

2010
01:19:08,698 --> 01:19:11,308
- This is the real jjajang rabokki. - It's fantastic.

2011
01:19:11,738 --> 01:19:12,868
Rabokki.

2012
01:19:12,868 --> 01:19:15,008
Gosh, the smell of jjajang...

2013
01:19:15,838 --> 01:19:17,208
is rising up.

2014
01:19:18,978 --> 01:19:20,448
(Adding udon next)

2015
01:19:20,448 --> 01:19:22,578
Oh, my.

2016
01:19:23,178 --> 01:19:24,218
This is good.

2017
01:19:24,218 --> 01:19:26,188
(The noodles are getting cooked while absorbing the broth.)

2018
01:19:26,188 --> 01:19:28,158
- Gosh. - This is to die for.

2019
01:19:29,288 --> 01:19:30,658
Gosh, this is crazy.

2020
01:19:32,758 --> 01:19:33,928
It really looks like...

2021
01:19:33,928 --> 01:19:35,098
- a tteokbokki place. - That's crazy.

2022
01:19:35,098 --> 01:19:36,398
Gosh, the pickled radish looks good.

2023
01:19:36,398 --> 01:19:37,928
The seasoned pickled radish.

2024
01:19:38,568 --> 01:19:39,728
(A full BJGRU rabokki spread)

2025
01:19:39,728 --> 01:19:41,298
It looks as good as a restaurant in Sindang-dong.

2026
01:19:41,298 --> 01:19:42,298
No, a restaurant in Sindang-dong...

2027
01:19:42,298 --> 01:19:43,298
- is much better. - Seriously.

2028
01:19:43,298 --> 01:19:45,608
It's what you buy with the money you earned,

2029
01:19:45,608 --> 01:19:47,908
- so it's a privilege of a grown-up. - That's right.

2030
01:19:48,038 --> 01:19:49,638
The ramyeon and udon I like.

2031
01:19:49,638 --> 01:19:50,648
You should've added glass noodles...

2032
01:19:50,648 --> 01:19:52,008
- to that. - And garaetteok.

2033
01:19:52,678 --> 01:19:55,148
But glass noodles absorb the soup too much.

2034
01:19:55,148 --> 01:19:56,148
- I see. - Gosh.

2035
01:19:56,318 --> 01:19:57,618
- The chili oil... - He isn't listening to her again.

2036
01:19:58,748 --> 01:20:00,088
He doesn't approve of her at all.

2037
01:20:00,618 --> 01:20:03,558
- Sung Hwan, will you follow me out? - No, that's not true.

2038
01:20:03,688 --> 01:20:05,328
- I... No! - I'll just whisper in your ear.

2039
01:20:05,328 --> 01:20:07,328
- I'm saying it absorbs soup a bit. - He never accepts it.

2040
01:20:07,328 --> 01:20:08,728
- Spare me a moment, Sung Hwan. - He never listens.

2041
01:20:09,228 --> 01:20:11,468
- Come out to the hallway for a bit. - But...

2042
01:20:11,468 --> 01:20:13,228
There's a spot with no security camera.

2043
01:20:13,768 --> 01:20:15,268
Gosh, it's all set.

2044
01:20:15,268 --> 01:20:17,908
(BJGRU rabokki is ready without a flaw.)

2045
01:20:18,808 --> 01:20:21,038
(Gulping)

2046
01:20:22,178 --> 01:20:24,978
That's crazy. The garaetteok looks crazy good.

2047
01:20:26,918 --> 01:20:28,518
(Taking a bite)

2048
01:20:32,588 --> 01:20:34,418
(Nodding in approval)

2049
01:20:35,018 --> 01:20:36,318
It's so good.

2050
01:20:40,428 --> 01:20:43,098
(This time, he picks up a handful of noodles.)

2051
01:20:43,298 --> 01:20:44,398
It looks so good.

2052
01:20:45,128 --> 01:20:46,628
Gosh, he mixed the noodles.

2053
01:20:46,628 --> 01:20:48,768
- That's udon and ramyeon. - And ramyeon.

2054
01:20:48,768 --> 01:20:49,768
- And ramyeon. - They have different textures.

2055
01:20:49,768 --> 01:20:51,368
- It's udon and jjajangmyeon. - That's right.

2056
01:20:51,808 --> 01:20:53,638
You should've added glass noodles to the combination.

2057
01:20:53,878 --> 01:20:56,948
He doesn't want to. He doesn't like glass noodles.

2058
01:20:56,948 --> 01:20:58,978
- No! I'll try it next time. - You don't like them?

2059
01:20:59,708 --> 01:21:02,618
You should get one raw noodle and chew it in front of her.

2060
01:21:02,618 --> 01:21:04,618
I'll chew them in front of you.

2061
01:21:04,888 --> 01:21:06,018
To that texture... Gosh.

2062
01:21:06,018 --> 01:21:07,118
(They seem to be unable to reach an agreement on glass noodles.)

2063
01:21:07,618 --> 01:21:09,718
(Taking a bite of the udon and ramyeon combination)

2064
01:21:10,728 --> 01:21:12,388
(Slurping)

2065
01:21:12,388 --> 01:21:13,498
- I'm jealous. - That must be good.

2066
01:21:14,428 --> 01:21:16,158
I'm so hungry.

2067
01:21:16,158 --> 01:21:17,468
(Slurping)

2068
01:21:18,198 --> 01:21:19,468
(Glaring)

2069
01:21:19,468 --> 01:21:21,168
He got a spark in his eyes.

2070
01:21:24,538 --> 01:21:26,878
That's it. One should eat like that.

2071
01:21:26,878 --> 01:21:29,138
The udon and ramyeon combination sounds good.

2072
01:21:31,878 --> 01:21:33,208
Personally,

2073
01:21:33,618 --> 01:21:35,418
there's a number I'm confident with. It's 100.

2074
01:21:35,918 --> 01:21:37,618
"How heavy are you?" "I weigh 100kg."

2075
01:21:37,618 --> 01:21:38,948
It gives off confidence.

2076
01:21:39,218 --> 01:21:40,558
"I weigh around 90kg."

2077
01:21:40,558 --> 01:21:41,788
When I say that, I don't look so good.

2078
01:21:41,788 --> 01:21:43,318
That's my opinion.

2079
01:21:43,318 --> 01:21:45,258
So when I do pull-ups,

2080
01:21:45,258 --> 01:21:46,958
it looks cool when I'm 100kg.

2081
01:21:46,958 --> 01:21:50,528
I weigh around 94kg now.

2082
01:21:50,528 --> 01:21:52,698
When I did trekking, I weighed around 95kg.

2083
01:21:53,328 --> 01:21:56,468
Before, I'd hit 100kg in no time,

2084
01:21:56,838 --> 01:21:58,708
but now, it's hard to go back to 100kg.

2085
01:21:59,238 --> 01:22:01,308
I think the number 100 is cool.

2086
01:22:01,308 --> 01:22:02,938
(The cool number 100 he can't seem to reach)

2087
01:22:02,938 --> 01:22:04,148
What a thing to worry about.

2088
01:22:06,208 --> 01:22:08,248
To you, a man ought to...

2089
01:22:08,248 --> 01:22:10,748
- weigh 100kg? - Yes, from 100kg to 105kg.

2090
01:22:12,918 --> 01:22:15,418
(Slurping aggressively)

2091
01:22:22,228 --> 01:22:23,328
(A little bit more...)

2092
01:22:23,328 --> 01:22:24,428
You should stop at 100kg.

2093
01:22:24,428 --> 01:22:25,428
(Worried)

2094
01:22:26,698 --> 01:22:29,398
(Chewing tenaciously to reach 100kg)

2095
01:22:30,908 --> 01:22:32,568
- You finished it? - Of course.

2096
01:22:32,908 --> 01:22:35,108
It's only a few bites.

2097
01:22:38,808 --> 01:22:40,778
(Taking something)

2098
01:22:41,478 --> 01:22:43,248
You must wrap up with fried rice.

2099
01:22:45,318 --> 01:22:46,348
- Goodness. - You know your stuff.

2100
01:22:46,348 --> 01:22:48,388
Nice! I was almost disappointed.

2101
01:22:48,388 --> 01:22:49,458
- Gosh, it's nice. - Nice.

2102
01:22:51,658 --> 01:22:52,958
Laver flakes!

2103
01:22:52,958 --> 01:22:54,998
(Sprinkling laver flakes as well)

2104
01:22:56,858 --> 01:22:59,528
You become really smart when you eat.

2105
01:22:59,528 --> 01:23:01,338
- He's a genius in that field. - I know.

2106
01:23:05,508 --> 01:23:07,238
(Grabbing)

2107
01:23:09,678 --> 01:23:10,748
What is that?

2108
01:23:10,748 --> 01:23:11,748
(Curious)

2109
01:23:12,378 --> 01:23:14,018
I use that when I press pork rinds.

2110
01:23:14,018 --> 01:23:16,018
That's the pork rind presser!

2111
01:23:16,578 --> 01:23:18,588
(He knows how to eat.)

2112
01:23:18,588 --> 01:23:20,148
(Amazed)

2113
01:23:20,148 --> 01:23:21,988
- That's right. - Now we're on the same page.

2114
01:23:21,988 --> 01:23:23,158
(A dramatic agreement)

2115
01:23:23,158 --> 01:23:24,628
When you make scorched rice,

2116
01:23:25,088 --> 01:23:26,728
- For real? - if you use that...

2117
01:23:26,728 --> 01:23:29,358
You can use that to make hotteok at home.

2118
01:23:29,358 --> 01:23:30,828
When you grill pork rinds, they pop,

2119
01:23:30,828 --> 01:23:33,368
- so you press them with that. - I knew it.

2120
01:23:33,368 --> 01:23:34,598
You have that at home too?

2121
01:23:34,598 --> 01:23:35,998
- I do. - Of course she does.

2122
01:23:36,768 --> 01:23:39,108
- They're on the same page. - Everyone has an expertise.

2123
01:23:40,238 --> 01:23:41,738
(Pressing hard)

2124
01:23:44,748 --> 01:23:45,878
That's it.

2125
01:23:47,218 --> 01:23:48,778
My love, cheese.

2126
01:23:49,748 --> 01:23:51,018
- I love adding cheese. - Cheese!

2127
01:23:51,018 --> 01:23:54,158
(If you add plenty of cheese on top,)

2128
01:23:55,288 --> 01:23:57,828
(you get fried rice worth 100 points!)

2129
01:23:58,158 --> 01:24:01,028
(Happy)

2130
01:24:02,698 --> 01:24:04,928
I wonder how it'd taste with cheese on top of jjajang teokbokki.

2131
01:24:06,268 --> 01:24:10,438
(I should eat it before it gets cold.)

2132
01:24:14,078 --> 01:24:16,738
(Gosh, it's hot.)

2133
01:24:18,708 --> 01:24:21,518
A tiger moth flies into the flame...

2134
01:24:21,518 --> 01:24:23,788
because it knows it's drawn to it.

2135
01:24:24,218 --> 01:24:25,688
I wanted to eat it,

2136
01:24:26,018 --> 01:24:27,758
so without realizing,

2137
01:24:28,118 --> 01:24:30,958
I took a bite despite knowing that it'd burn me.

2138
01:24:32,758 --> 01:24:33,928
He's flying into it.

2139
01:24:34,498 --> 01:24:36,198
(Swallowing)

2140
01:24:37,528 --> 01:24:39,028
It's too hot.

2141
01:24:39,028 --> 01:24:40,228
(Shocked)

2142
01:24:42,438 --> 01:24:46,168
(Despite that, the 95kg tiger moth flies into it.)

2143
01:24:47,008 --> 01:24:48,778
(Only if I can reach the cool weight of 100kg...)

2144
01:24:48,778 --> 01:24:50,138
He has a goal of reaching 100kg, that's why.

2145
01:24:52,008 --> 01:24:53,108
(Groaning)

2146
01:24:53,448 --> 01:24:55,578
- It must be good. - Because it's too good...

2147
01:24:56,018 --> 01:24:58,348
It's only delicious when you have it hot.

2148
01:25:00,688 --> 01:25:03,728
(Scraping)

2149
01:25:05,328 --> 01:25:07,058
The last bit. The scorched rice.

2150
01:25:09,058 --> 01:25:12,768
(The last spurt toward 100kg)

2151
01:25:13,668 --> 01:25:14,898
Where did it all go?

2152
01:25:16,538 --> 01:25:19,638
(Without realizing it, he finished the fried rice.)

2153
01:25:20,308 --> 01:25:24,248
(Gosh, that was a good meal.)

2154
01:25:25,008 --> 01:25:27,348
(Here we go.)

2155
01:25:28,618 --> 01:25:32,718
(After the meal, he lies down on the wooden platform.)

2156
01:25:36,118 --> 01:25:37,628
(Putting his leg up)

2157
01:25:38,558 --> 01:25:41,728
(Enjoying his free time with a gentle breeze)

2158
01:25:41,728 --> 01:25:43,598
- That's true happiness. - I love it.

2159
01:25:45,468 --> 01:25:47,738
A day that felt familiar yet completely new.

2160
01:25:47,938 --> 01:25:50,168
It was really strange. I felt good.

2161
01:25:51,408 --> 01:25:53,538
Actually, for a few months,

2162
01:25:54,738 --> 01:25:56,108
I was a bit...

2163
01:25:56,108 --> 01:25:58,878
(Emotionally, he was a little...)

2164
01:25:58,878 --> 01:26:01,148
I had a hard time emotionally.

2165
01:26:01,278 --> 01:26:05,748
It could be because I got new things...

2166
01:26:05,748 --> 01:26:07,188
and the environment changed.

2167
01:26:08,588 --> 01:26:11,358
I'm somewhat excited.

2168
01:26:11,628 --> 01:26:13,628
I feel like there will be more good days to come.

2169
01:26:14,058 --> 01:26:15,458
It was 100 percent good.

2170
01:26:15,458 --> 01:26:19,028
(It was 100 percent good.)

2171
01:26:22,868 --> 01:26:26,638
(A 100-point Ordinary Day)

2172
01:26:26,638 --> 01:26:28,178
(Next story)

2173
01:26:29,178 --> 01:26:30,278
(Exclaiming)

2174
01:26:30,978 --> 01:26:33,308
(The rookie at living alone, Gyeong Hye,)

2175
01:26:33,308 --> 01:26:34,478
(dove into independent life.)

2176
01:26:35,618 --> 01:26:36,878
(She's busily going to work today as well?)

2177
01:26:37,918 --> 01:26:40,318
(Peeking around)

2178
01:26:40,418 --> 01:26:42,558
(She's arrived at a construction site?)

2179
01:26:42,618 --> 01:26:44,888
(The cafe I used to work at closed down.)

2180
01:26:45,728 --> 01:26:49,398
(Seeing that by myself gave me mixed emotions.)

2181
01:26:51,228 --> 01:26:53,128
(Why am I choking up?)

2182
01:26:53,998 --> 01:26:55,938
(Hello.)

2183
01:26:56,598 --> 01:26:57,838
(It's a bummer.)

2184
01:26:57,838 --> 01:27:00,838
(She cheerfully says goodbye to the next door store owners.)

2185
01:27:01,908 --> 01:27:03,808
(Don't you need a part-timer here?)

2186
01:27:04,348 --> 01:27:05,448
(She looks for a job while she's at it.)

2187
01:27:05,448 --> 01:27:06,508
(I do need a part-timer!)

2188
01:27:06,908 --> 01:27:08,178
(Lively)

2189
01:27:08,918 --> 01:27:10,278
(What she's improved...)

2190
01:27:10,278 --> 01:27:11,888
(is her trying to do things by herself.)

2191
01:27:12,648 --> 01:27:14,888
(I'm the one who delved into the rough life.)

2192
01:27:14,888 --> 01:27:16,118
What do I do?

2193
01:27:16,758 --> 01:27:18,928
(I'm going to do everything that I want to do.)

2194
01:27:19,528 --> 01:27:21,028
(I'm missing a part.)

2195
01:27:21,028 --> 01:27:22,498
(Is it here?)

2196
01:27:22,758 --> 01:27:23,868
(Thump)

2197
01:27:24,068 --> 01:27:25,168
(Thud)

2198
01:27:25,698 --> 01:27:26,898
(Will you hold it for me, Wilson?)

2199
01:27:27,468 --> 01:27:29,098
(Sweating buckets)

2200
01:27:29,168 --> 01:27:31,038
(Fine. I can just shower four times.)

2201
01:27:32,938 --> 01:27:36,208
(I'm the Queen of Living Alone)

2202
01:27:37,138 --> 01:27:39,248
(Panting)

2203
01:27:39,648 --> 01:27:42,118
(Dae Ho is working out in the mountains?)

2204
01:27:43,078 --> 01:27:45,988
(Recently, I got a health checkup.)

2205
01:27:46,418 --> 01:27:49,858
(Dae Ho's health and daily routine have fallen apart lately.)

2206
01:27:50,118 --> 01:27:51,658
(To become healthier,)

2207
01:27:51,658 --> 01:27:53,728
(I wanted to make changes.)

2208
01:27:55,128 --> 01:27:56,558
Goodness.

2209
01:27:56,958 --> 01:27:57,968
(The first step toward a change)

2210
01:27:57,968 --> 01:27:59,468
(Cleaning the house)

2211
01:28:00,098 --> 01:28:02,498
(It's as clean as a new house.)

2212
01:28:02,938 --> 01:28:05,108
(Nana, you're happy that it's cleaner, right?)

2213
01:28:08,778 --> 01:28:10,848
(Drilling into the wall?)

2214
01:28:11,308 --> 01:28:13,448
(So he can work out as soon as he gets up,)

2215
01:28:13,648 --> 01:28:15,818
(he installs a home climbing wall.)

2216
01:28:16,518 --> 01:28:19,888
(It's what I want to do.)

2217
01:28:20,148 --> 01:28:22,758
(I can do anything to become healthy.)

2218
01:28:23,458 --> 01:28:25,488
(A fool who only cares about health)

2219
01:28:25,958 --> 01:28:27,228
(Taking a step toward...)

2220
01:28:27,228 --> 01:28:29,028
(healthier tomorrow)

2221
01:28:29,028 --> 01:28:30,198
(Let's go!)

2222
01:28:30,668 --> 01:28:33,228
(I feel healthy already.)

2223
01:28:33,828 --> 01:28:38,208
(Tomorrow, Dae Ho Will Be Healthy)
